--- what this run shows, and what it does not -------------------------------------------
SHOWN (the incident's exact request):
  • build A's worker precached 68 entries (66 manifest files + '/' + the CV) on first visit.
  • after the deploy the origin answers build A's asset with HTTP 404 — the incident.
  • the page, still holding build A's document, asks for it and the WORKER answers
    HTTP 200 with 224 bytes from fm-static-4879b1e7. That is the rescue path
    (fetch → 404 → matchAcrossGenerations) executing end to end in a real browser.
  • build A's generation was still present after the reload: it was not deleted out from
    under the page running build A, which is what the old activate() did.
  • build B's page: sections 6, canvases 1, error shell false.

NOT SHOWN in this run:
  • build B's worker did not reach activate inside the window (fm-static-fdad9403 absent,
    and the synthetic third generation fm-static-deadbeef was therefore never collected),
    so the "keeps exactly two, drops the rest" half was not observed in the browser here.
    That logic is asserted statically instead — tests/sw_strategy.test.mjs,
    "activate keeps the previous generation alive" (4 assertions, all passing in
    04-tests-passing.log). Worth a follow-up run that waits on the registration update
    rather than on a cache name.
  • the A-only probe is a _buildManifest.js rather than a numbered lazy chunk: the trailing
    comment used to force build B was removed by minification, so the GLCanvas chunk hash
    was identical in both builds. Same worker code path (/_next/static 404 → cross-generation
    lookup); a differently-hashed lazy chunk would exercise no additional branch.
